<section>
  <h2>A Generative Model of Contact Locations</h2>
  <p>
    Different contact locations and forces can produce the same joint torque and base force/torque measurements. CDM models this ambiguity as a
    distribution instead of selecting a single explanation. Its diffusion model generates contact-point samples conditioned on proprioceptive
    measurements, previous estimates, and the geometry of the robot surface.
  </p>
  <figure class="research-figure--compact">
    <a href="{{ '/assets/img/research/cdm/introduction.webp' | relative_url }}">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/img/research/cdm/introduction.webp' | relative_url }}"
        alt="Contact diffusion estimates a distribution of possible contact points from proprioceptive sensors"
        loading="lazy"
      >
    </a>
    <figcaption>Generated samples represent the possible locations of one or two contacts.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  {% include research-project-video.liquid path="cdm/cdm_singularity" caption="Representing multiple plausible contact configurations when sensor observations are ambiguous." %}
</section>

<section>
  <h2>Temporal Context and Surface Geometry</h2>
  <p>
    A short observation window can lose information about an earlier contact once a second contact occurs. CDM conditions on an earlier single-contact
    estimate to retain this context. A contact-state classifier controls when that historical estimate is updated, and the model can also run without
    a historical estimate.
  </p>
  <figure class="research-figure--compact">
    <a href="{{ '/assets/img/research/cdm/dataset.webp' | relative_url }}">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/img/research/cdm/dataset.webp' | relative_url }}"
        alt="Sequential single-contact, transition dual-contact, and steady dual-contact training observations"
        loading="lazy"
      >
    </a>
    <figcaption>Sequential contact scenarios provide the training data for historical conditioning.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Signed distance fields describe the robot's link surfaces and guide generated points toward physically meaningful locations. The denoising network
    uses these surface features alongside sensor measurements and historical diffusion outputs. Each sample uses 64 points to represent the contact
    distribution.
  </p>
  <figure class="research-figure--wide">
    <a href="{{ '/assets/img/research/cdm/network_structure.webp' | relative_url }}">
      <img
        src="{{ '/assets/img/research/cdm/network_structure.webp' | relative_url }}"
        alt="CDM architecture showing the denoising loop, contact classifier, historical conditioning, and signed distance fields"
        loading="lazy"
      >
    </a>
    <figcaption>The model combines an iterative denoiser, surface constraints, and contact-state-dependent historical conditioning.</figcaption>
  </figure>
